    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and it passes along with the full build.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` on owner creation \u2014 the number of existing owners (before the create) sharing the same first and last name, case-insensitively. The value is captured at create time and persisted, so a later `GET /api/owners/{id}` returns the snapshot.\n\nChanges, each kept in the unit where it belongs:\n\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `namesakeCount` (int32, `minimum: 0`) property to the `Owner` response schema. The generator produces `OwnerDto.namesakeCount`, which MapStruct maps automatically by name.\n- **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter, alongside the other create-time attributes like `customerCode` and `householdId`.\n- **`rest/validation/NamesakeCounter.java`** (new) \u2014 a small single-responsibility component that counts existing owners matching a given first+last name case-insensitively, following the same in-memory `findAllOwners()` pattern as `OwnerHouseholdUniquenessValidator` (stored names aren't normalized, so a DB query can't match reliably).\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 injected `NamesakeCounter` and set the count on the new owner just before saving, so it reflects owners existing *before* this create.\n- **`db/{hsqldb,h2,postgres,mysql}/schema.sql`** \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column (tests run on hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so the schema is authoritative; the other dialects are kept in sync).\n\n`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
